Zambia’s second-term government is taking shape with a familiar hand on the economy, even as the political temperature remains high over detentions, election petitions, and a government pushback against civil society criticism. Meanwhile, a major solar investment and progress on mineral mapping offer tangible signs of the Grow Zambia Agenda moving beyond rhetoric, though the proof will be in execution and delivery.

## Main Stories

1. **Musokotwane returns to Finance, tasked with delivering growth** – President Hichilema has reappointed Situmbeko Musokotwane as Minister of Finance and National Planning, alongside Mulambo Haimbe (Foreign Affairs), Gilbert Siame (Green Economy), and Roma Chilengi (Health). The President has shifted the mandate from stabilisation to results, urging ministers to protect public funds and make growth felt by ordinary citizens. Stakeholders have welcomed the continuity, but the pressure is now on Dr Musokotwane to translate macroeconomic gains into tangible improvements – a promise that has yet to be fully realised.

2. **Nevers Mumba quits active politics, redefines service** – New Nation Party leader Nevers Mumba has resigned from his party position and the UPND Alliance Council of Presidents, saying Zambia needs him differently in this hour. He has handed leadership to Reuben Sambo and framed his move as a “redefinition of service,” positioning himself as a counsellor to the President. The timing – just weeks into the new term – raises questions about the stability of the opposition alliance and whether this signals a broader realignment or a quiet exit.

3. **Attorney-General rejects CSO claims of lawlessness** – Mulilo Kabesha has dismissed civil society assertions that Zambia has descended into lawlessness, insisting the government remains committed to constitutionalism and human rights. He defended the cancellation of passports, arrests on treason and sedition charges, and the detention of Mafinga South MP Faith Munthali, who has since been released on police bond. The government’s response is firm, but the lack of independent verification on key cases – and the sub judice nature of the charges – leaves the public with more questions than answers.

4. **Zesco signs $519m solar deal with Hungarian firm** – Zesco and Enersynk Group have signed a power purchase agreement for a 500MW solar project in Copperbelt Province, with the first 300MW targeted for operation within 16 months. Half of the environmental credits will fund a community trust. This is a significant vote of confidence in Zambia’s energy sector, but the 12-month timeline to financial close and the 25-year agreement’s execution will determine whether this translates into reliable power for Zambians.

## Other Notable Stories

**Mining and Energy:** Zambia’s high-resolution geophysical survey is now 93.6% complete, with the Ministry of Mines saying it will unlock new exploration opportunities. Chinese officials have pledged continued support for critical minerals development, aligning with the global energy transition.

**Governance and Law:** The Nkana election petition has heard allegations of intimidation and vote buying from a UPND monitor, with the matter continuing before Judge Charles Zulu. Meanwhile, the Zambia Revenue Authority has tightened tax clearance rules to curb evasion and shell-company arrangements, a move that could affect businesses that have not registered for all applicable tax types.

**Local Government:** The Lusaka City Council has secured K40 million for flood mitigation and drainage expansion, with the Mayor urging residents to stop dumping rubbish in drains. The funds are welcome, but the city’s history of flooding suggests the challenge is as much about behaviour change as infrastructure.

**Defence and Remembrance:** Zambia’s first indigenous Air Force commander, Lt Gen Peter Dingiswayo Zuze, has died at 83. He is being remembered for his role in securing the nation during a challenging period in its history.

**Economy and Budget:** Cabinet has approved the Budget Policy Concept Paper for the 2027-2029 Medium-Term Budget Plan, prioritising growth, single-digit inflation, and fiscal consolidation. The Finance Minister has also called for stronger tax compliance and enforcement to address leakages from smuggling and evasion.

**Corporate and Community:** Konkola Copper Mines has highlighted its US$20 million annual community investment through an independent trust, alongside a US$5 million, five-year partnership with the Football Association of Zambia. The company frames this as long-term investment, but the public will be watching whether these commitments are independently verified and sustained.

**Education and Technology:** Airtel Africa has launched a Tech Fellowship, awarding scholarships to students at the University of Zambia and Copperbelt University to support the next generation of technology talent.

**Justice:** In a curious case, a Lumezi man accused of driving off with a police car has told the Chipata High Court he has never driven a car in his life. Robert Mapili’s defence – that he cannot drive and was not at the scene – will be tested as the trial continues.

## Key Takeaways & Watchpoints

* **Cabinet formation and delivery:** With only four ministers sworn in so far, watch for the full Cabinet list and whether the President follows through on his warning against non-performing officers. The 2027 Budget, guided by the approved policy paper, will be the first test of whether the Grow Zambia Agenda is properly funded.
* **Election petitions and political tensions:** The Nkana petition and others will test the credibility of the electoral process. Watch for court timelines and whether the government’s handling of detained opposition figures – including MP Faith Munthali’s case – fuels further civil society criticism or eases tensions.
* **Energy and mining execution:** The Zesco-Enersynk deal and the completion of the mineral mapping survey are promising, but the 12-month financial close deadline and the actual rollout of the solar project will determine whether Zambia’s energy and mining ambitions are realised or remain on paper.